## 3. Assets: logo and images

### 3.1 Logo

The logo is already supplied by the client as an image uploaded to this chat. Use it exactly as-is: no redrawing, no recolouring, no reinterpreting, no re-typesetting the wordmark.

- Save it into `/images` as `true-blue-yard-maintenance-logo.png` (if the file is not already in the project folder, ask the person to drop it in before continuing).
- What it is: a navy square with five leaves (three sage green, two pale blue-white) arranged in a loose circle, then "True Blue" in bold sage green and "Yard Maintenance" in a light weight pale tone underneath.
- **The logo carries its own solid navy background.** Place it only on navy surfaces (header, hero, footer) so it blends in, or in a contained square with rounded corners on light sections. Never put it on white as a floating mark with a mismatched navy box and no intent.
- Sample the exact hex values for navy, green and pale blue straight from the file with a script (Python PIL is fine) and use those as the palette anchors in section 5.
- Generate a favicon (`/images/favicon.png`, 64 px) by cropping to the leaf mark only.
- Generate an Open Graph image (`/images/og-image.jpg`, 1200 by 630) with the logo centred on the sampled navy.

### 3.2 Photos (sourcing order, the client chose all of these)

The client confirmed photos come from **their Google Business Profile, Facebook, Instagram, and stock images as a supplement.**

1. **Their own work photos first.** Google (Photos, "By owner") and their Facebook and Instagram (53 posts) are the primary source. Their own photos of their own jobs are fair game on their own site.
2. **Facebook and Instagram block automated fetching.** If you cannot open them, do not work around it with scrapers, mirrors or caches. Stop and tell the person to drop the photos into a folder called `/images/incoming`, then carry on from there. Check that folder at the start in case photos are already in it.
3. **Stock images are a supplement only**, for atmosphere in hero or section backgrounds (close-up grass texture, a mower edge, hedge detail). Rules:
   - Properly licensed only (Unsplash or Pexels style licences), subjects must be real Australian-looking lawns and gardens, never anything with a competing brand or logo visible.
   - **Stock is never shown on the Gallery page and never captioned or implied as True Blue's work.** The Gallery is real jobs only.
   - Record the source URL and licence for every stock image in an HTML comment next to where it is used.
4. If you end up with fewer than 8 real work photos, build the Gallery with what exists and say so in the final QA report. Do not pad it with stock.

### 3.3 Image pipeline

- Inventory every image. Look at each one and identify what it actually shows (mown stripes, edged path, hedge before and after, trailer of green waste, pressure washed driveway).
- Rename to `lowercase-kebab-case-descriptive.jpg`. No camera names, no `IMG_1234`, no `photo1`.
- Create a `.webp` version alongside each original. Use `<picture>` with the webp source and jpg fallback.
