## 5. Design direction

### 5.1 Clichés to avoid

- No warm-cream background with a terracotta/clay accent and a big serif headline.
- No near-black background with a single neon-green or acid accent used everywhere.
- No hairline-rule "broadsheet" layout with dense newspaper columns.
- No centered hero with a gradient-blob background and a generic stock photo of unrelated smiling people.
- No numbered "01 / 02 / 03" feature cards unless there's a real sequence being described.
- No emoji in copy or UI, no generic icon-pack icons standing in for real photography.
- No filler marketing copy: "world-class," "premier," "unlock your potential," "passionate about delivering excellence." Every claim needs to be something you could actually back up from the snapshot in section 2.
- Also avoid the stereotypical "lawn care green gradient with a cartoon mower" look. This is a navy-led brand.

### 5.2 Colour palette (anchored to the real logo)

Sample the true values from the logo file and replace the approximations below. Define all as CSS custom properties in `:root`. Names and roles:

| Token | Approx value | Why it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| `--navy` | #244B6E | The logo's own background. Header, hero, footer. This is the "True Blue". |
| `--navy-deep` | #17334D | A darker step of the same navy for alternating sections and depth. |
| `--leaf` | #6F9C64 | The logo's sage green. Stripes, rules, large display accents. |
| `--leaf-bright` | #9CC78F | A lifted green for text and buttons on navy. The plain sage fails contrast on navy for small text, so this carries the readable green. |
| `--mist` | #DCE7EE | The pale blue-white leaf. Panels, chips, soft dividers. |
| `--paper` | #F4F8FA | Cool near-white page background for light sections. Cool, not cream. |

Check WCAG AA on every text and background pairing you actually use. Adjust `--leaf-bright` and text colours until they pass. Body text on light sections is `--navy-deep`.

### 5.3 Typography

- **Display and headings:** Lexend, weights 700 and 300. The logo wordmark is a clean geometric sans with a heavy line over a light line, and Lexend carries that exact contrast, so headings feel like the logo grew into a website.
- **Body:** Source Sans 3, 400 and 600. Sturdy, easy to read on a phone in a driveway.
- Scale: large confident hero headline, restrained body size (17 to 18 px), generous line height.
- Use the heavy-over-light pairing deliberately: a bold line followed by a light line in key headings, echoing the logo lockup.

### 5.4 Signature element (spend the boldness here, everything else stays disciplined)

**The mown-stripe band.** A band of alternating `--leaf` and slightly darker green stripes, exactly like a freshly mown lawn, made in pure CSS (`repeating-linear-gradient`) at a slight diagonal. It sits at the bottom of the hero and is cut with a hard, perfectly straight edge into the next section, like a trimmed lawn edge. On first scroll into view the stripes wipe in once, left to right.

The same straight-edge cut is reused as the section divider on the other pages, but quietly (a thin stripe strip, no animation). Nothing else on the site gets a flashy treatment.

### 5.5 Motion

- Deliberate and sparse: the one stripe wipe, a gentle scroll reveal on section content, and hover and focus states.
- Fully respect `prefers-reduced-motion`: when set, no motion at all (stripes appear already drawn, content visible immediately), not just slower motion.

### 5.6 Quality floor (always)

Fully responsive down to a 360 px phone. Visible keyboard focus states on every interactive element. Real descriptive alt text on every image. Semantic HTML5 throughout (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `section`, `footer`, one `h1` per page, correct heading order). Tap targets at least 44 px. Most visitors will arrive from Google on a phone, so design phone first.

